  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
